Topic: Underdispersion, positive responses, and quasi-likelihood · more questions on this topic
Source: Practice set, question 55 of 59 ‹ Q54 Q56 ›

[6 marks]
A researcher measures the time Y_i&gt;0 taken for a chemical reaction to complete at temperature x_i. The reaction times are positively skewed, and their variability appears to increase as the mean reaction time increases. They fit
glm(time ~ temperature, family = Gamma(link = "log"))
(a) Write down explicitly the statistical model corresponding to this R code. Include:

(b) the conditional distribution of Y_i;
(c) the mean \mu_i;
(d) the variance of Y_i;
(e) the link between \mu_i and the linear predictor.

[2 marks]
(f) Suppose the fitted coefficient of temperature is \hat\beta_1=-0.08. Interpret this coefficient on the mean response scale. [2 marks]
(g) Why might a Gamma response distribution be more sensible here than a normal response distribution? [1 mark]
(h) Under this model, how does \operatorname{Var}(Y_i\mid x_i) change as the conditional mean \mu_i increases? [1 mark]






Worked answer (click to show)





Definitions. A \operatorname{Gamma}(a,b) variable with shape a and rate b has mean a/b and variance a/b^2. Here \mu_i=\mathbb{E}(Y_i\mid x_i) and \phi&gt;0 is the common dispersion. The log link models the mean through \eta_i=\log\mu_i=\beta_0+\beta_1x_i; it does not assert a distribution for \log Y_i. Temperatures are fixed and the responses conditionally independent.
(a) Explicit model statement. Treat the temperatures as fixed. For i=1,\ldots,n, assume conditionally independent responses with \boxed{
Y_i\mid x_i\overset{\mathrm{ind}}{\sim}
\operatorname{Gamma}\!\left(
\text{shape}=\frac1\phi,\quad
\text{rate}=\frac1{\phi\mu_i}\right),\qquad Y_i&gt;0,
} where \phi&gt;0 is a common dispersion parameter. The log link gives \boxed{\log\mu_i=\eta_i=\beta_0+\beta_1x_i,
\qquad\mu_i=\exp(\beta_0+\beta_1x_i).} Here \eta_i is the linear predictor and \beta_0,\beta_1\in\mathbb R. A Gamma distribution with shape a and rate b has mean a/b and variance a/b^2. Substituting the parameters above gives \boxed{\mathbb{E}[Y_i\mid x_i]=\mu_i,\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(Y_i\mid x_i)=\phi\mu_i^2.} Equivalently, the Gamma scale is \phi\mu_i. State the parameterisation: the mean \mu_i is not the Gamma shape parameter.
(b) Temperature effect on the mean response scale. For a one-unit increase in the recorded temperature, \frac{\hat\mu(x+1)}{\hat\mu(x)}
=\frac{\exp\{\hat\beta_0+\hat\beta_1(x+1)\}}
       {\exp(\hat\beta_0+\hat\beta_1x)}
=e^{-0.08}\approx0.9231. Thus each additional temperature unit is associated with a fitted mean reaction time that is multiplied by 0.9231, or is approximately \boxed{100(1-e^{-0.08})\%\approx7.69\%\text{ lower}.} This is a proportional change in the expected time, not a decrease of 0.08 time units. An 8% decrease is only a first-order approximation.
(c) Why a Gamma response? Reaction time is positive and continuous. A Gamma response has this support, permits right skewness, and accommodates larger variance at larger means. An ordinary constant-variance normal model is symmetric, allows negative times, and does not capture the stated mean–variance pattern. These features motivate Gamma regression; they do not by themselves prove that it fits adequately.
(d) How the variance changes. For fixed \phi, variance increases quadratically with the mean: \operatorname{Var}(Y_i\mid x_i)=\phi\mu_i^2. Multiplying the mean by k&gt;0 multiplies the variance by k^2; in particular, doubling the mean quadruples the variance. The conditional standard deviation is \sqrt\phi\,\mu_i, so the coefficient of variation is constant at \sqrt\phi.

Topic: Introduction to generalised linear models · more questions on this topic
Source: Practice set, question 33 of 59 ‹ Q32 Q34 ›

[6 marks]
For the model in Question 32, let S(\boldsymbol\beta)
=\frac{\partial\ell(\boldsymbol\beta)}{\partial\boldsymbol\beta}.
(a) Derive the score vector S(\boldsymbol\beta).
(b) Show that the score has expectation zero under the assumed model.
(c) Calculate the covariance matrix of the score and the Hessian of the log-likelihood separately. Hence verify that \operatorname{Var}\!\left(S(\boldsymbol\beta)\mid X\right)
        =-\mathbb{E}\!\left[
        \frac{\partial^2\ell(\boldsymbol\beta)}
        {\partial\boldsymbol\beta\,\partial\boldsymbol\beta^\top}
        \,\middle|\,X\right].






Worked answer (click to show)





Definitions. Let \boldsymbol\beta\in\mathbb R^p and let \mathbf X_i be a p-dimensional column vector, so the ith row of X is \mathbf X_i^\top. Write \eta_i=\mathbf X_i^\top\boldsymbol\beta, \mu_i=e^{\eta_i}, and W=\operatorname{diag}(\mu_1,\ldots,\mu_n). The score is S=\partial\ell/\partial\boldsymbol\beta and the Hessian is H=\partial^2\ell/(\partial\boldsymbol\beta\,\partial\boldsymbol\beta^\top). Fisher information is -\mathbb{E}(H\mid X).
All expectations and covariances below are conditional on the fixed design X and evaluated under the same parameter \boldsymbol\beta as the score.
(a) Score vector. From Answer 32, \ell(\boldsymbol\beta)
=\sum_{i=1}^n\{Y_i\eta_i-e^{\eta_i}\}+C. For component r, \frac{\partial\eta_i}{\partial\beta_r}=X_{ir},
\qquad
\frac{\partial\mu_i}{\partial\beta_r}=\mu_iX_{ir}. Consequently, S_r(\boldsymbol\beta)
=\sum_{i=1}^n(Y_i-\mu_i)X_{ir}, or in vector form, \boxed{
S(\boldsymbol\beta)
=\sum_{i=1}^n\mathbf X_i(Y_i-\mu_i)
=X^\top(\mathbf Y-\boldsymbol\mu).
}
(b) Expected score. The Poisson model gives \mathbb{E}(Y_i\mid X)=\mu_i. Hence \boxed{
\mathbb{E}\{S(\boldsymbol\beta)\mid X\}
=\sum_{i=1}^n\mathbf X_i\{\mathbb{E}(Y_i\mid X)-\mu_i\}
=\mathbf0.
} This is a statement about the expected score under the assumed model; the score from an arbitrary observed sample need not be zero at the true parameter.
(c) Covariance of the score. Set \varepsilon_i=Y_i-\mu_i. Then \mathbb{E}(\varepsilon_i\mid X)=0,
\qquad
\mathbb{E}(\varepsilon_i\varepsilon_j\mid X)
=\begin{cases}
\mu_i,&i=j,\\
0,&i\ne j,
\end{cases} using the Poisson variance and conditional independence. Since the score has expectation zero, \begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Var}\{S(\boldsymbol\beta)\mid X\}
&=\mathbb{E}\{S(\boldsymbol\beta)S(\boldsymbol\beta)^\top\mid X\}\\
&=\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n
\mathbf X_i\mathbb{E}(\varepsilon_i\varepsilon_j\mid X)\mathbf X_j^\top\\
&=\sum_{i=1}^n\mu_i\mathbf X_i\mathbf X_i^\top
=X^\top WX.
\end{aligned}
Hessian, calculated separately. Differentiate the rth score component with respect to \beta_s: H_{rs}(\boldsymbol\beta)
=\frac{\partial S_r}{\partial\beta_s}
=-\sum_{i=1}^n\mu_iX_{is}X_{ir}. Thus \boxed{
H(\boldsymbol\beta)
=\frac{\partial^2\ell}
       {\partial\boldsymbol\beta\,\partial\boldsymbol\beta^\top}
=-\sum_{i=1}^n\mu_i\mathbf X_i\mathbf X_i^\top
=-X^\top WX.
} For fixed X and \boldsymbol\beta, this Hessian does not depend on \mathbf Y. Therefore -\mathbb{E}\{H(\boldsymbol\beta)\mid X\}=X^\top WX, which matches the covariance calculated above: \boxed{
\operatorname{Var}\{S(\boldsymbol\beta)\mid X\}
=-\mathbb{E}\left[
\frac{\partial^2\ell}
     {\partial\boldsymbol\beta\,\partial\boldsymbol\beta^\top}
\,\middle|\,X\right]
=X^\top WX.
}

Key result. The information identity is verified by two calculations: the covariance of the score and the negative expected Hessian. Simply asserting that they are equal does not supply the requested verification.

The zero expected score uses the correct conditional mean. The information equality additionally uses Poisson variances and independence: overdispersion or correlation changes the score covariance even if the mean remains correct.

Topic: Bayesian hypotheses and model comparison · more questions on this topic
Source: Practice set, question 21 of 59 ‹ Q20 Q22 ›

[4 marks]
An analysis produces a Bayes factor of 4 in favour of model M_1 over model M_0. Before observing the data, the researcher regarded M_0 as nine times as probable as M_1. The researcher concludes:

“The Bayes factor favours M_1, so M_1 must now have the greater posterior probability.”

Assess this conclusion. Explain how the Bayes factor changes the model odds, and identify which model has the greater posterior probability.






Worked answer (click to show)





Definitions. The Bayes factor is B_{10}=p({\boldsymbol{x}}\mid M_1)/p({\boldsymbol{x}}\mid M_0). Odds in favour of M_1 are \mathbb P(M_1)/\mathbb P(M_0), with the analogous conditional ratio for posterior odds. The two models exhaust the comparison, so their probabilities sum to one. Here B_{10}=4 and the prior odds are 1:9.
A Bayes factor updates prior model odds according to \frac{\mathbb{P}(M_1\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})}{\mathbb{P}(M_0\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})}
=B_{10}
\frac{\mathbb{P}(M_1)}{\mathbb{P}(M_0)}. Because M_0 was initially nine times as probable as M_1, the prior odds in favour of M_1 are \frac{\mathbb{P}(M_1)}{\mathbb{P}(M_0)}=\frac19. With B_{10}=4, the posterior odds become \boxed{
\frac{\mathbb{P}(M_1\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})}{\mathbb{P}(M_0\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})}
=4\left(\frac19\right)=\frac49.
} Therefore, \mathbb{P}(M_1\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})=\frac4{13},
\qquad
\mathbb{P}(M_0\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})=\frac9{13}. The data favour M_1 relative to the prior because B_{10}&gt;1, but the evidence is not strong enough to overcome the initial nine-to-one odds in favour of M_0. Thus \boxed{
\mathbb{P}(M_0\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})&gt;
\mathbb{P}(M_1\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}).
} The researcher’s conclusion is therefore incorrect: a Bayes factor favouring M_1 does not necessarily make M_1 the more probable model a posteriori.

Topic: Bayesian foundations · more questions on this topic
Source: Practice set, question 13 of 59 ‹ Q12 Q14 ›

In the Second World War, Allied statisticians estimated German tank production from the serial numbers on captured tanks. Treat the serial numbers as continuous. Suppose X_1,\ldots,X_n\mid\theta
\overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}\operatorname{Uniform}(0,\theta),
\qquad \theta&gt;0, and write x_{(n)}=\max_i x_i. Place the Pareto prior p(\theta\mid\alpha,\beta)
=\frac{\alpha\beta^\alpha}{\theta^{\alpha+1}},
\qquad \theta\geq\beta, where \alpha,\beta&gt;0. Here \beta is a known lower bound on production.
[13 marks]
(a) Write the likelihood with every indicator function shown explicitly. Derive the posterior distribution of \theta and show that it is Pareto. State its parameters, and state exactly when the data change the prior lower bound. [4 marks]
(b) Find the posterior mean. The MLE is \widehat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}=x_{(n)}. Explain why the posterior mean always exceeds it, and why this is sensible rather than a sign of bias in the Bayesian estimate. [2 marks]
(c) Derive the posterior predictive density of one new captured serial number \widetilde X. Show that it is a two-component mixture, and identify both components and their weights. Find \mathbb{P}\!\left(
          \widetilde X&gt;\max\{\beta,x_{(n)}\}\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}
        \right) and interpret it. [4 marks]
(d) The Pitman–Koopman–Darmois theorem links sufficient statistics of fixed dimension to exponential families. Here x_{(n)} is a one-dimensional sufficient statistic for every n, and a conjugate prior exists. Explain why this does not make \operatorname{Uniform}(0,\theta) an exponential family, and why it does not contradict the theorem. [2 marks]
(e) The captured serials are 19,40,42,60, and the prior has \alpha=1 and \beta=50. Give the posterior mean and a 95% highest-posterior-density interval for \theta. Justify the lower endpoint of the interval. [1 mark]






Worked answer (click to show)





Definitions. Write x_{(1)}=\min_i x_i and x_{(n)}=\max_i x_i; \mathbf1\{A\} is one when A holds and zero otherwise. Our Pareto convention is p(\theta)=ab^a\theta^{-a-1}\mathbf1\{\theta\ge b\}, with shape a&gt;0 and lower bound b&gt;0. \widetilde X denotes a new serial number, conditionally independent of the sample given \theta. We use the stated continuous, conditionally iid model with n\ge1 and positive observations.
Write a_n=\alpha+n,
\qquad
b_n=\max\{\beta,x_{(n)}\}.

(a) Likelihood and posterior
For observations from \operatorname{Uniform}(0,\theta), \begin{aligned}
L(\theta;{\boldsymbol{x}})
&=\prod_{i=1}^n
  \left[\frac{1}{\theta}\,
  \mathbf{1}\{0&lt;x_i&lt;\theta\}\right]\\
&=\theta^{-n}\,
  \mathbf{1}\{x_{(1)}&gt;0\}
  \prod_{i=1}^n\mathbf{1}\{x_i&lt;\theta\}\\
&=\theta^{-n}\,
  \mathbf{1}\{x_{(1)}&gt;0\}
  \mathbf{1}\{x_{(n)}&lt;\theta\}.
\end{aligned} For a valid observed sample, \mathbf{1}\{x_{(1)}&gt;0\} does not depend on \theta. Hence L(\theta;{\boldsymbol{x}})
\propto\theta^{-n}\mathbf{1}\{\theta&gt;x_{(n)}\}. Multiplying by the prior gives \begin{aligned}
p(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
&\propto
\theta^{-n}\mathbf{1}\{\theta&gt;x_{(n)}\}
\theta^{-(\alpha+1)}\mathbf{1}\{\theta\geq\beta\}\\
&=\theta^{-(\alpha+n+1)}
  \mathbf{1}\{\theta\geq b_n\}\\
&=\theta^{-(a_n+1)}\mathbf{1}\{\theta\geq b_n\}.
\end{aligned} The distinction between strict and non-strict inequalities at one endpoint is irrelevant for a continuous density. Since \int_{b_n}^{\infty}\theta^{-(a_n+1)}\,d\theta
=\frac{b_n^{-a_n}}{a_n}, the normalised posterior density is p(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
=\frac{a_n b_n^{a_n}}{\theta^{a_n+1}},
\qquad \theta\geq b_n. Therefore, \boxed{
\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}
\sim\operatorname{Pareto}\!\left(
\alpha+n,\max\{\beta,x_{(n)}\}
\right).
} The data change the lower bound exactly when x_{(n)}&gt;\beta. Otherwise the lower bound remains \beta, although the shape still changes from \alpha to \alpha+n.


(b) Posterior mean
Because a_n=\alpha+n&gt;1, the posterior mean exists and is \boxed{
\mathbb{E}(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
=\frac{a_n b_n}{a_n-1}
=\frac{(\alpha+n)\max\{\beta,x_{(n)}\}}
{\alpha+n-1}.
} Since a_n/(a_n-1)&gt;1 and b_n\geq x_{(n)}, \mathbb{E}(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})&gt;b_n\geq x_{(n)}
=\widehat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}. This is sensible. The MLE is the smallest value of \theta compatible with the data because the likelihood decreases for \theta&gt;x_{(n)}. Moreover, X_{(n)}&lt;\theta almost surely and \mathbb{E}(X_{(n)}\mid\theta)=\frac{n}{n+1}\theta, so the sample maximum systematically falls below the true upper endpoint. The posterior mean allows for the unobserved region above the largest captured serial. Frequentist bias is a repeated-sampling property; the posterior mean is instead the Bayes estimate under squared-error loss.


(c) Posterior predictive distribution
For a new serial number \widetilde X, \begin{aligned}
p(\widetilde x\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
&=\int_{b_n}^{\infty}
  \frac{1}{\theta}\mathbf{1}\{0&lt;\widetilde x&lt;\theta\}
  \frac{a_n b_n^{a_n}}{\theta^{a_n+1}}\,d\theta\\
&=a_n b_n^{a_n}
  \int_{\max\{b_n,\widetilde x\}}^{\infty}
  \theta^{-(a_n+2)}\,d\theta,
  \qquad \widetilde x&gt;0.
\end{aligned} Thus p(\widetilde x\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
=
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle
\frac{a_n}{a_n+1}\frac{1}{b_n},
&0&lt;\widetilde x\leq b_n,\\[1.1em]
\displaystyle
\frac{a_n b_n^{a_n}}{(a_n+1)\widetilde x^{a_n+1}},
&\widetilde x&gt;b_n,\\[0.8em]
0,&\widetilde x\leq0.
\end{cases} Equivalently, this is the mixture \boxed{
\frac{a_n}{a_n+1}\operatorname{Uniform}(0,b_n)
+\frac{1}{a_n+1}\operatorname{Pareto}(a_n,b_n).
} The component weights sum to one. The Uniform component has no probability above b_n, whereas the Pareto component lies above b_n almost surely. Therefore, \boxed{
\mathbb{P}(\widetilde X&gt;b_n\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
=\frac{1}{a_n+1}
=\frac{1}{\alpha+n+1}.
} This is the posterior predictive probability that the next captured serial exceeds whichever is larger: the confirmed lower bound \beta or the current sample maximum.


(d) Why there is no contradiction with the Pitman–Koopman–Darmois theorem
In a regular exponential-family model, the support cannot change with the parameter: a representation of the form p(x\mid\theta)
=b(x)\frac{\exp\{c(\theta)T(x)\}}{a(\theta)} has support determined by b(x), since the exponential term is positive. For the Uniform model, p(x\mid\theta)=\frac{1}{\theta}
\mathbf{1}\{0&lt;x&lt;\theta\}, and the indicator switches on or off as \theta changes. Its support depends on \theta, so this is an irregular model rather than a regular exponential family.
The Pitman–Koopman–Darmois theorem assumes, among other regularity conditions, parameter-independent support. This model violates that assumption and lies outside the theorem’s scope. It may therefore have the fixed-dimensional sufficient statistic x_{(n)} and a conjugate prior without being an exponential family. The existence of a conjugate prior does not by itself imply exponential-family structure.


(e) Numerical posterior and 95% HPD interval
Here n=4, x_{(n)}=60, \alpha=1, and \beta=50, so a_n=5,
\qquad
b_n=60,
\qquad
\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}\sim\operatorname{Pareto}(5,60). The posterior mean is \boxed{
\mathbb{E}(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})=\frac{5(60)}{4}=75.
} The posterior density is strictly decreasing on [60,\infty), so its highest-density interval must begin at the posterior mode and lower support boundary, 60. If the upper endpoint is u, then 0.05
=\mathbb{P}(\theta&gt;u\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
=\left(\frac{60}{u}\right)^5. Hence u=60(20)^{1/5}\approx109.2, and \boxed{
\text{95\% HPD interval}
=\left[60,\,60(20)^{1/5}\right]
\approx[60,109.2].
} The lower endpoint is 60 because values below the largest observed serial are impossible, and the decreasing posterior density is maximised at that boundary.

Topic: Conjugate analysis for normal models · more questions on this topic
Source: Book exercise from Conjugate analysis for normal models ‹ Full conditionals Detailed balance ›

A pair (r,s) has a normal–inverse-\chi^2 distribution, written (r,s)\sim N\text{-}\chi^{-2}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta), if

p(r,s)\propto
s^{-(\gamma+3)/2}
\exp\!\left[-\frac{\beta(r-\alpha)^2+\gamma\delta}{2s}\right],
\qquad r\in\mathbb{R},\quad s&gt;0,

where \beta,\gamma,\delta&gt;0. Let X_i\mid\mu,\sigma^2\stackrel{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}
\operatorname{N}(\mu,\sigma^2) for i=1,\ldots,n and put the prior

(\mu,\sigma^2)\sim
N\text{-}\chi^{-2}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta).

Show that the joint posterior has the same family and identify all four updated parameters. Your derivation should explicitly complete the two weighted squares in \mu.






Worked answer (click to show)





Question. A pair (r,s) has a normal–inverse-\chi^2 distribution, written (r,s)\sim N\text{-}\chi^{-2}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta), if

p(r,s)\propto
s^{-(\gamma+3)/2}
\exp\!\left[-\frac{\beta(r-\alpha)^2+\gamma\delta}{2s}\right],
\qquad r\in\mathbb{R},\quad s&gt;0,

where \beta,\gamma,\delta&gt;0. Let X_i\mid\mu,\sigma^2\stackrel{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}
\operatorname{N}(\mu,\sigma^2) for i=1,\ldots,n and put the prior

(\mu,\sigma^2)\sim
N\text{-}\chi^{-2}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta).

Show that the joint posterior has the same family and identify all four updated parameters. Your derivation should explicitly complete the two weighted squares in \mu.
Solution. Write

\overline x=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n x_i,
\qquad
s_x^2=\frac1{n-1}\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\overline x)^2.

The sum-of-squares decomposition is

\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\mu)^2
=(n-1)s_x^2+n(\overline x-\mu)^2.

Hence the likelihood kernel is \begin{align*}
p({\boldsymbol{x}}\mid\mu,\sigma^2)
&\propto(\sigma^2)^{-n/2}
\exp\!\left[-\frac{1}{2\sigma^2}
\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\mu)^2\right]\\
&=(\sigma^2)^{-n/2}
\exp\!\left[-\frac{(n-1)s_x^2+n(\overline x-\mu)^2}
{2\sigma^2}\right].
\end{align*} Multiplication by the prior gives \begin{align*}
p(\mu,\sigma^2\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
&\propto
(\sigma^2)^{-n/2}
\exp\!\left[-\frac{(n-1)s_x^2+n(\overline x-\mu)^2}
{2\sigma^2}\right]\\
&\quad\times
(\sigma^2)^{-(\gamma+3)/2}
\exp\!\left[-\frac{\beta(\mu-\alpha)^2+\gamma\delta}
{2\sigma^2}\right]\\
&=(\sigma^2)^{-(n+\gamma+3)/2}
\exp\!\left[-\frac{
(n-1)s_x^2+n(\mu-\overline x)^2+
\beta(\mu-\alpha)^2+\gamma\delta}
{2\sigma^2}\right].
\end{align*}
For positive u,v, direct expansion verifies

u(z-r)^2+v(z-t)^2
=(u+v)\left(z-\frac{ur+vt}{u+v}\right)^2
+\frac{uv}{u+v}(r-t)^2.

Taking u=n, v=\beta, z=\mu, r=\overline x, and t=\alpha yields \begin{align*}
n(\mu-\overline x)^2+\beta(\mu-\alpha)^2
&=(n+\beta)(\mu-\alpha^\ast)^2
+\frac{n\beta}{n+\beta}(\overline x-\alpha)^2,
\end{align*} where

\alpha^\ast=\frac{n\overline x+\beta\alpha}{n+\beta}.

Define \begin{align*}
\beta^\ast&=\beta+n,\\
\gamma^\ast&=\gamma+n,\\
\delta^\ast
&=\frac1{\gamma^\ast}\left[
\gamma\delta+(n-1)s_x^2
+\frac{n\beta}{n+\beta}(\overline x-\alpha)^2
\right].
\end{align*} Then the posterior kernel becomes

p(\mu,\sigma^2\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
\propto
(\sigma^2)^{-(\gamma^\ast+3)/2}
\exp\!\left[-\frac{
\beta^\ast(\mu-\alpha^\ast)^2+\gamma^\ast\delta^\ast}
{2\sigma^2}\right].

Thus

\boxed{(\mu,\sigma^2)\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}
\sim N\text{-}\chi^{-2}
(\alpha^\ast,\beta^\ast,\gamma^\ast,\delta^\ast)}.

Topic: Markov chains, Gibbs sampling, and Metropolis–Hastings · more questions on this topic
Source: Book exercise from Markov chains, Gibbs sampling, and Metropolis–Hastings ‹ Random-walk Metropolis for a correlated normal Beta hidden inside Dirichlet ›

Suppose the target is \operatorname{Gamma}_{\mathrm{sc}}(\alpha,\beta) with shape \alpha and scale \beta. Describe an independent Metropolis–Hastings sampler using the proposal \operatorname{Gamma}_{\mathrm{sc}}(\gamma,\gamma/\alpha). Derive the Hastings ratio rather than merely quoting the generic formula.






Worked answer (click to show)





Question. Suppose the target is \operatorname{Gamma}_{\mathrm{sc}}(\alpha,\beta) with shape \alpha and scale \beta. Describe an independent Metropolis–Hastings sampler using the proposal \operatorname{Gamma}_{\mathrm{sc}}(\gamma,\gamma/\alpha). Derive the Hastings ratio rather than merely quoting the generic formula.
Solution. Under the shape–scale convention, the target and proposal densities have kernels \begin{align*}
\pi(z)&\propto z^{\alpha-1}e^{-z/\beta},\\
g(z)&\propto z^{\gamma-1}e^{-z/\lambda},
\qquad \lambda=\frac{\gamma}{\alpha},
\end{align*} for z&gt;0. The proposal does not depend on the current state, so this is an independence sampler. If the current state is x and Y\sim g is proposed, then \begin{align*}
r(x,Y)
&=\frac{\pi(Y)g(x)}{\pi(x)g(Y)}\\
&=\frac{Y^{\alpha-1}e^{-Y/\beta}}
{x^{\alpha-1}e^{-x/\beta}}
\frac{x^{\gamma-1}e^{-x/\lambda}}
{Y^{\gamma-1}e^{-Y/\lambda}}\\
&=\left(\frac{Y}{x}\right)^{\alpha-\gamma}
\exp\!\left[(Y-x)\left(\frac1\lambda-\frac1\beta\right)\right]\\
&=\boxed{
\left(\frac{Y}{x}\right)^{\alpha-\gamma}
\exp\!\left[(Y-x)\left(\frac{\alpha}{\gamma}
-\frac1\beta\right)\right]}.
\end{align*} Accept Y with probability \min\{1,r(x,Y)\}; otherwise retain x.






Parameterisation check



The main book writes \operatorname{Gamma}(a,b) in shape–rate form, whereas this exercise explicitly declares scale parameters. If the proposal’s second argument \gamma/\alpha were instead read as a rate, its kernel would be z^{\gamma-1}e^{-(\gamma/\alpha)z} and the final exponential coefficient would be \gamma/\alpha-1/\beta. The algorithm is not defined numerically until that convention is fixed.

Full Bayesian treatment
Let {\boldsymbol{\phi}}=(\alpha,\beta)^{\mathsf T}. A full Bayesian model assigns a hyperprior p(\alpha,\beta) and uses \begin{align*}
p({\boldsymbol{\theta}},\alpha,\beta)
&=p(\alpha,\beta)
   \prod_{j=1}^Jp(\theta_j\mid\alpha,\beta),\\
p({\boldsymbol{y}}\mid{\boldsymbol{\theta}},\alpha,\beta)
&=p({\boldsymbol{y}}\mid{\boldsymbol{\theta}})
=\prod_{j=1}^Jp(y_j\mid\theta_j).
\end{align*} The second equality follows because the data are conditionally independent of the hyperparameters given the study rates. Therefore 
\begin{aligned}
p({\boldsymbol{\theta}},\alpha,\beta\mid{\boldsymbol{y}})
&\propto
p(\alpha,\beta)
\prod_{j=1}^J
p(y_j\mid\theta_j)
p(\theta_j\mid\alpha,\beta).
\end{aligned}
\qquad(6)
For one study, the two factors are \begin{align*}
p(y_j\mid\theta_j)
&=\binom{n_j}{y_j}
   \theta_j^{y_j}(1-\theta_j)^{n_j-y_j},\\
p(\theta_j\mid\alpha,\beta)
&=\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+\beta)}
        {\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(\beta)}
   \theta_j^{\alpha-1}(1-\theta_j)^{\beta-1}.
\end{align*} Holding (\alpha,\beta) fixed and multiplying powers, \begin{align*}
p(\theta_j\mid\alpha,\beta,{\boldsymbol{y}})
&\propto
\theta_j^{y_j+\alpha-1}
(1-\theta_j)^{n_j-y_j+\beta-1}.
\end{align*}

Proposition 2 (Study-rate full conditional) Normalising gives 
\boxed{
\theta_j\mid\alpha,\beta,{\boldsymbol{y}}
\sim\operatorname{Beta}(y_j+\alpha,n_j-y_j+\beta)}.
\qquad(7) Conditional on the hyperparameters, the study rates are independent, so the joint conditional is the product of these J Beta densities.
Article: Study-rate full conditional →

Integrating out the study rates
To obtain the marginal posterior for (\alpha,\beta), integrate Equation 6 over every \theta_j. For one study, \begin{align*}
&\int_0^1p(y_j\mid\theta_j)
            p(\theta_j\mid\alpha,\beta)\,\mathrm{d}\theta_j\\
&\quad=
\binom{n_j}{y_j}
\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+\beta)}
      {\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(\beta)}
\int_0^1
\theta_j^{y_j+\alpha-1}
(1-\theta_j)^{n_j-y_j+\beta-1}\,\mathrm{d}\theta_j\\
&\quad=
\binom{n_j}{y_j}
\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+\beta)}
      {\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(\beta)}
B(y_j+\alpha,n_j-y_j+\beta)\\
&\quad=
\binom{n_j}{y_j}
\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+\beta)
       \Gamma(y_j+\alpha)
       \Gamma(n_j-y_j+\beta)}
      {\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(\beta)
       \Gamma(n_j+\alpha+\beta)}.
\end{align*}

Proposition 3 (Marginal hyperposterior) The binomial coefficient does not depend on (\alpha,\beta), so the hyperposterior kernel is 
\boxed{
p(\alpha,\beta\mid{\boldsymbol{y}})
\propto p(\alpha,\beta)
\prod_{j=1}^J
\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+\beta)
       \Gamma(y_j+\alpha)
       \Gamma(n_j-y_j+\beta)}
      {\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(\beta)
       \Gamma(n_j+\alpha+\beta)}}.
\qquad(8) This expression is available pointwise but is not a familiar normalised density.
Article: Marginal hyperposterior →

Conjugate update with every constant shown
Multiplying Equation 1 and Equation 2 gives \begin{align*}
  p(x\mid\theta)p(\theta)
  &=\binom{n}{x}\theta^x(1-\theta)^{n-x}
    \frac{\theta^{\alpha-1}(1-\theta)^{\beta-1}}
         {B(\alpha,\beta)}\\
  &=\frac{\binom{n}{x}}{B(\alpha,\beta)}
    \theta^{x+\alpha-1}
    (1-\theta)^{n-x+\beta-1}.
\end{align*} The integral of the last two factors is B(x+\alpha,n-x+\beta), so the evidence is 
\begin{aligned}
p(x)
  &=\int_0^1p(x\mid\theta)p(\theta)\,\mathrm{d}\theta\\
  &=\binom{n}{x}
    \frac{B(x+\alpha,n-x+\beta)}{B(\alpha,\beta)}.
\end{aligned}
\qquad(3) Dividing by this evidence cancels the binomial coefficient and the old Beta constant: \begin{align*}
  p(\theta\mid x)
  &=\frac{p(x\mid\theta)p(\theta)}{p(x)}\\
  &=\frac{
    \theta^{x+\alpha-1}(1-\theta)^{n-x+\beta-1}}
    {B(x+\alpha,n-x+\beta)}.
\end{align*}

Proposition 1 (Beta–Binomial conjugacy) Therefore 
\boxed{
  \theta\mid x\sim
  \operatorname{Beta}(\alpha+x,\beta+n-x)}.
\qquad(4)
Article: Beta–Binomial conjugacy →







Key idea



The update is “prior successes plus observed successes” and “prior failures plus observed failures.” The Beta family is conjugate to the binomial likelihood because the posterior is another Beta density.

