Properties

Bayes action for two hypotheses

Main article: Bayes action for two hypotheses

These probabilities do not by themselves define an action. If choosing action a_0 when H_1 is true costs L_{01} and choosing a_1 when H_0 is true costs L_{10}, the posterior expected losses are

\rho(a_0\mid x)=L_{01}\mathbb{P}(H_1\mid x),
\qquad
\rho(a_1\mid x)=L_{10}\mathbb{P}(H_0\mid x).

Choose a_0 when \begin{align*}
L_{01}\mathbb{P}(H_1\mid x)
&&lt;L_{10}\mathbb{P}(H_0\mid x)\\
\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\frac{\mathbb{P}(H_0\mid x)}{\mathbb{P}(H_1\mid x)}
&&gt;\frac{L_{01}}{L_{10}}.
\end{align*} Equal losses reduce the threshold to one. This separates the probability calculation from the decision rule.


Posterior-odds factorisation

Main article: Posterior-odds factorisation

For two models M_1 and M_2, Bayes’ rule gives

\mathbb{P}(M_j\mid x)
=\frac{p(x\mid M_j)\mathbb{P}(M_j)}{p(x)}.

Taking the ratio cancels the common evidence p(x): 
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathbb{P}(M_1\mid x)}{\mathbb{P}(M_2\mid x)}
&=\frac{p(x\mid M_1)\mathbb{P}(M_1)/p(x)}
         {p(x\mid M_2)\mathbb{P}(M_2)/p(x)}\\
&=\underbrace{\frac{p(x\mid M_1)}{p(x\mid M_2)}}_{
      \operatorname{BF}_{12}}
   \underbrace{\frac{\mathbb{P}(M_1)}{\mathbb{P}(M_2)}}_{
      \text{prior odds}}.
\end{aligned}

Corollary taught in Bayesian linear regression, part of Bayesian statistics
\boxed{
{\boldsymbol{\beta}}\mid{\boldsymbol{y}},{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}
\sim t_{n-p}\!\left(
\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}},
s^2({\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}})^{-1}\right),}
 where the displayed matrix is the multivariate t scale matrix. In particular, 
\beta_j\mid{\boldsymbol{y}},{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}
\sim t_{n-p}\!\left(\widehat\beta_j,s\sqrt{c_{jj}}\right),
 so an equal-tailed 100(1-\alpha)\% credible interval is 
\widehat\beta_j\ \pm\ t_{1-\alpha/2,n-p}s\sqrt{c_{jj}}.
 Its endpoints coincide with the usual classical interval under this reference prior. The interpretations do not coincide: the Bayesian statement concerns posterior uncertainty about \beta_j for the observed data, whereas the frequentist statement concerns repeated-sample coverage by a random interval.

