Reading the addiction model
Category 0 is weak-willed, category 1 is addiction by disease, category 2 is both. From the fitted predictors, \begin{align*}
\log\frac{\hat p_1}{\hat p_0}
&=-1.161+0.437\,\mathrm{female}+0.0299\,\mathrm{age}+1.622\,\mathrm{university},\\
\log\frac{\hat p_2}{\hat p_0}
&=-2.016+0.288\,\mathrm{female}+0.0421\,\mathrm{age}+1.067\,\mathrm{university}.
\end{align*} A female, relative to a male with the same age and the same university status, has odds of disease-versus-weak-willed multiplied by \exp(0.437)=1.55. The z statistic is 2.25, so that contrast is past the usual 5% line. The same comparison for both-versus-weak-willed is \exp(0.288)=1.33, with z=1.30, which we would not call a detected sex difference.
Age is a per-year coefficient. \exp(0.0299)=1.03 looks small until you remember that the unit is one year. Ten years is 10\times 0.0299=0.299 on the log-odds scale, and \exp(0.299)\approx 1.35, not 1.03. Both age slopes are clearly away from zero (z=4.80 and 6.17). The per-year sentence and the per-decade sentence are the same fit.
Being a university academic multiplies the odds of disease-versus-weak-willed by \exp(1.622)=5.06, and the odds of both-versus-weak-willed by \exp(1.067)=2.91. Those are the largest contrasts in the table (z=6.40 and 3.69).
Any other pair is the difference of two rows. The log-odds of both versus disease alone use 
\log\frac{p_2}{p_1}
=(-2.016+1.161)+(0.288-0.437)\,\mathrm{female}+(0.0421-0.0299)\,\mathrm{age}+(1.067-1.622)\,\mathrm{university}.
 You do not refit. The multinomial dispersion stays 1 as long as each person makes one independent choice of one category.

Unknown variance, known mean
Now assume \mu is known and \sigma^2 is unknown. Define

  S_\mu=\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\mu)^2.

The likelihood as a function of \sigma^2 is \begin{align*}
p({\boldsymbol{x}}\mid\sigma^2)
&=(2\pi\sigma^2)^{-n/2}
   \exp\!\left(-\frac{S_\mu}{2\sigma^2}\right)\\
&\propto
   (\sigma^2)^{-n/2}
\exp\!\left(-\frac{S_\mu}{2\sigma^2}\right).
\end{align*}

Finding the conjugate family by inspection
It is more useful to see how one might discover the prior than simply to be given its name. The unknown \sigma^2 is positive and continuous, and the likelihood kernel contains two distinctive pieces:

(\sigma^2)^{-n/2}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\exp\!\left\{-\frac{S_\mu/2}{\sigma^2}\right\}.

We therefore want a positive continuous density that already contains a power of \sigma^2 and an exponential in 1/\sigma^2.
The natural candidates can be rejected or repaired one at a time. An exponential density is positive and continuous, but its kernel e^{-\lambda\sigma^2} has \sigma^2, rather than 1/\sigma^2, in the exponent; with one rate parameter it also has its mode fixed at zero. A \chi^2 distribution is only a special Gamma distribution. A Gamma density for \sigma^2 contains (\sigma^2)^{a-1}e^{-b\sigma^2}, so both appearances of \sigma^2 point the wrong way. A Gamma density for the precision \tau=1/\sigma^2, however, has exactly the required structure. Transforming back gives the inverse-Gamma family.

Definition 2 (Inverse-Gamma distribution) Under the book’s convention,

\sigma^2\sim\operatorname{InvGamma}(\alpha_0,\beta_0),

means that \sigma^2&gt;0 has density

p(\sigma^2)
=\frac{\beta_0^{\alpha_0}}{\Gamma(\alpha_0)}
  (\sigma^2)^{-\alpha_0-1}
\exp\!\left(-\frac{\beta_0}{\sigma^2}\right).

Article: Inverse-Gamma distribution →

This follows directly from the transformation \tau=1/\sigma^2\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(\alpha_0,\beta_0). Indeed,

\frac{\,\mathrm{d}\tau}{\,\mathrm{d}\sigma^2}=-\frac1{(\sigma^2)^2},
\qquad
\left|\frac{\,\mathrm{d}\tau}{\,\mathrm{d}\sigma^2}\right|
=\frac1{(\sigma^2)^2},

and therefore \begin{align*}
p(\sigma^2)
&=p_\tau(1/\sigma^2)
   \left|\frac{\,\mathrm{d}\tau}{\,\mathrm{d}\sigma^2}\right|\\
&=\frac{\beta_0^{\alpha_0}}{\Gamma(\alpha_0)}
   (1/\sigma^2)^{\alpha_0-1}
   e^{-\beta_0/\sigma^2}
   \frac1{(\sigma^2)^2}\\
&=\frac{\beta_0^{\alpha_0}}{\Gamma(\alpha_0)}
   (\sigma^2)^{-\alpha_0-1}e^{-\beta_0/\sigma^2}.
\end{align*}
The posterior kernel is \begin{align*}
p(\sigma^2\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
&\propto p({\boldsymbol{x}}\mid\sigma^2)p(\sigma^2)\\
&\propto
(\sigma^2)^{-n/2}
e^{-S_\mu/(2\sigma^2)}
(\sigma^2)^{-\alpha_0-1}
e^{-\beta_0/\sigma^2}\\
&=(\sigma^2)^{-(\alpha_0+n/2)-1}
\exp\!\left[-\frac{\beta_0+S_\mu/2}{\sigma^2}\right].
\end{align*}

Proposition 2 (Normal–inverse-Gamma variance update) Hence 
\boxed{
\sigma^2\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}
\sim\operatorname{InvGamma}\!\left(
   \alpha_0+\frac n2,
   \beta_0+\frac12S_\mu
\right)}.
\qquad(3)
Article: Normal–inverse-Gamma variance update →

For T\sim\operatorname{InvGamma}(a,b),

  \mathbb{E}(T)=\frac b{a-1}\quad(a&gt;1),
  \qquad
  \operatorname{mode}(T)=\frac b{a+1}.

The posterior mode is therefore \begin{align*}
\widehat{\sigma^2}_{\mathrm{MAP}}
&=\frac{\beta_0+S_\mu/2}{\alpha_0+n/2+1}\\
&=\frac{2\beta_0+S_\mu}{2(\alpha_0+1)+n}\\
&=\frac{2(\alpha_0+1)}{2(\alpha_0+1)+n}
    \frac{\beta_0}{\alpha_0+1}
  +\frac{n}{2(\alpha_0+1)+n}
    \frac{S_\mu}{n}.
\end{align*} Thus, for the mode, the prior is equivalent to 2(\alpha_0+1) squared-deviation observations with total sum of squares 2\beta_0.
Always check the parameterisation before trusting a Gamma or inverse-Gamma formula. Shape–rate, shape–scale, variance, standard deviation, and precision conventions can produce expressions that look nearly identical while meaning different things. This is not a notational nuisance to wave away: it changes the numerical posterior.

Both normal parameters unknown
Consider the independent prior

  \mu\sim\operatorname{N}(\mu_0,\sigma_0^2),
  \qquad
  \sigma^2\sim\operatorname{InvGamma}(\alpha_0,\beta_0),
  \qquad \mu\perp\sigma^2.

This prior has two coordinates, so a request to software for its mean should return two numbers, not four. When \alpha_0&gt;1,

\mathbb{E}\!\begin{pmatrix}\mu\\ \sigma^2\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}\mu_0\\ \beta_0/(\alpha_0-1)\end{pmatrix}.

If the required second moments exist, the covariance matrix is diagonal under the prior because \mu and \sigma^2 are independent. This statement does not survive the update: the joint posterior in Equation 4 couples them.
With both parameters unknown, the joint posterior is 
\begin{aligned}
p(\mu,\sigma^2\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
&\propto p({\boldsymbol{x}}\mid\mu,\sigma^2)
          p(\mu)p(\sigma^2)\\
&\propto
(\sigma^2)^{-n/2}
\exp\!\left[-\frac1{2\sigma^2}
   \sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\mu)^2\right]\\
&\qquad\times
\exp\!\left[-\frac{(\mu-\mu_0)^2}{2\sigma_0^2}\right]
(\sigma^2)^{-\alpha_0-1}
\exp\!\left(-\frac{\beta_0}{\sigma^2}\right)\\
&\propto
(\sigma^2)^{-(\alpha_0+n/2)-1}
\exp\!\left[-\frac{\beta_0+\frac12\sum_i(x_i-\mu)^2}
                    {\sigma^2}
              -\frac{(\mu-\mu_0)^2}{2\sigma_0^2}\right].
\end{aligned}
\qquad(4) This joint density is not a product of independent posterior marginals. It is also not the most common textbook normal–inverse-Gamma family, because that family usually scales the prior variance of \mu by \sigma^2. Nevertheless, both full conditional distributions are standard.

Proposition 3 (Full conditionals under independent priors) Holding \sigma^2 fixed, the calculation from Equation 1 applies verbatim: 
\boxed{
\mu\mid\sigma^2,{\boldsymbol{x}}
\sim\operatorname{N}\!\left(
\frac{n\overline x\,\sigma_0^2+\mu_0\sigma^2}
      {n\sigma_0^2+\sigma^2},
\frac{\sigma^2\sigma_0^2}{n\sigma_0^2+\sigma^2}
\right)}.
\qquad(5) Holding \mu fixed, the calculation from Equation 3 applies: 
\boxed{
\sigma^2\mid\mu,{\boldsymbol{x}}
\sim\operatorname{InvGamma}\!\left(
\alpha_0+\frac n2,
\beta_0+\frac12\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\mu)^2
\right)}.
\qquad(6)
Article: Full conditionals under independent priors →

