Dirichlet–Multinomial conjugacy
Multiply the fully normalised likelihood and prior: \begin{align*}
p({\boldsymbol{x}}\mid{\boldsymbol{p}})p({\boldsymbol{p}})
&=\frac{n!}{\prod_kx_k!}
   \prod_kp_k^{x_k}
   \frac1{B({\boldsymbol{\alpha}})}
   \prod_kp_k^{\alpha_k-1}\\
&=\frac{n!}{\prod_kx_k!B({\boldsymbol{\alpha}})}
   \prod_kp_k^{x_k+\alpha_k-1}.
\end{align*} The integral over the simplex of the product is B({\boldsymbol{\alpha}}+{\boldsymbol{x}}), so 
\begin{aligned}
p({\boldsymbol{x}})
&=\frac{n!}{\prod_kx_k!}
   \frac{B({\boldsymbol{\alpha}}+{\boldsymbol{x}})}
        {B({\boldsymbol{\alpha}})}\\
&=\frac{n!}{\prod_kx_k!}
   \frac{\Gamma(\alpha_0)}{\Gamma(\alpha_0+n)}
   \prod_{k=1}^K
   \frac{\Gamma(\alpha_k+x_k)}{\Gamma(\alpha_k)}.
\end{aligned}
\qquad(4)

Theorem 1 (Dirichlet–Multinomial conjugacy) After division by this evidence, 
\boxed{
{\boldsymbol{p}}\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}
\sim\operatorname{Dirichlet}(\alpha_1+x_1,\ldots,\alpha_K+x_K)}.
\qquad(5)
Article: Dirichlet–Multinomial conjugacy →

The posterior mean is \begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}(P_k\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
&=\frac{\alpha_k+x_k}{\alpha_0+n}\\
&=\frac{\alpha_0}{\alpha_0+n}
    \frac{\alpha_k}{\alpha_0}
  +\frac n{\alpha_0+n}\frac{x_k}{n}.
\end{align*} The posterior mode, when every \alpha_k+x_k&gt;1, is

\widehat p_{k,\mathrm{MAP}}
=\frac{x_k+\alpha_k-1}{n+\alpha_0-K}.

Thus \alpha_k behaves like a pseudo-count for the mean and \alpha_k-1 for the mode.
For a new categorical observation, \begin{align*}
\mathbb{P}(Y_{n+1}=k\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
&=\int\mathbb{P}(Y_{n+1}=k\mid{\boldsymbol{p}})
       p({\boldsymbol{p}}\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})\,\mathrm{d}{\boldsymbol{p}}\\
&=\int p_kp({\boldsymbol{p}}\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})\,\mathrm{d}{\boldsymbol{p}}\\
&=\mathbb{E}(P_k\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})\\
&=\frac{\alpha_k+x_k}{\alpha_0+n}.
\end{align*}

Conjugate priors from the exponential-family form
Not every likelihood has a useful finite-dimensional conjugate family. A large and important class that does is the exponential family. Keeping the same notation, one observation has density or mass function 
p(x\mid\theta)
=\frac{b(x)\exp\{c(\theta)^{\mathsf T}T(x)\}}
        {a(\theta)},
\qquad(5) where T(x) is the one-observation natural statistic, c(\theta) is the canonical parameter, b(x) does not depend on \theta, and a(\theta) is the normalising function from Equation 3.
Choose the prior kernel, where \alpha has the same dimension as c(\theta) and \beta is scalar, 
p(\theta\mid\alpha,\beta)
\propto
\frac{\exp\{c(\theta)^{\mathsf T}\alpha\}}
      {a(\theta)^\beta}.
\qquad(6) For independent data x_1,\ldots,x_n, the likelihood is \begin{align*}
p({\boldsymbol{x}}\mid\theta)
&=\prod_{i=1}^n
   \frac{b(x_i)\exp\{c(\theta)^{\mathsf T}T(x_i)\}}
        {a(\theta)}\\
&=\left(\prod_{i=1}^nb(x_i)\right)
   \frac{
   \exp\{c(\theta)^{\mathsf T}S({\boldsymbol{x}})\}}
   {a(\theta)^n}.
\end{align*} Multiplying by the prior gives \begin{align*}
p(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
&\propto
\frac{\exp\{c(\theta)^{\mathsf T}S({\boldsymbol{x}})\}}
      {a(\theta)^n}
\frac{\exp\{c(\theta)^{\mathsf T}\alpha\}}
      {a(\theta)^\beta}\\
&=\frac{
   \exp\{c(\theta)^{\mathsf T}
     (\alpha+S({\boldsymbol{x}}))\}}
   {a(\theta)^{\beta+n}}.
\end{align*}

Proposition 2 (Generic exponential-family conjugate update) Thus the hyperparameters update as 
\boxed{
\alpha\leftarrow
\alpha+S({\boldsymbol{x}}),
\qquad
\beta\leftarrow\beta+n.}
\qquad(7)
