The residual sum-of-squares decomposition
The key step is to rewrite \operatorname{SSE}({\boldsymbol{\beta}}) around its minimiser. The point is to turn the “sum of squares for a potential model” into the fitted residual sum of squares plus a nonnegative quadratic penalty for moving away from \widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}.
For any coefficient displacement {\boldsymbol{\delta}}, full column rank gives the equivalent readings 
{\boldsymbol{\delta}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}{\boldsymbol{\delta}}
=\lVert{\boldsymbol{\delta}}\rVert_{{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}}^2
=\lVert{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}{\boldsymbol{\delta}}\rVert_2^2.
\qquad(16) Thus {\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}} measures separation between coefficient vectors by the separation they produce between fitted mean vectors. Directions in which the fitted surface changes rapidly are geometrically longer.
Begin with the candidate residual vector: \begin{align*}
{\boldsymbol{y}}-{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}{\boldsymbol{\beta}}
&={\boldsymbol{y}}-{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}
   +{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}{\boldsymbol{\beta}}\\
&=\widehat{{\boldsymbol{e}}}
   +{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-{\boldsymbol{\beta}}).
\end{align*} Its squared length is \begin{align*}
\operatorname{SSE}({\boldsymbol{\beta}})
&=\left[\widehat{{\boldsymbol{e}}}
    +{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-{\boldsymbol{\beta}})\right]^\top
   \left[\widehat{{\boldsymbol{e}}}
    +{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-{\boldsymbol{\beta}})\right]\\
&=\left\lVert\widehat{{\boldsymbol{e}}}
    +{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-{\boldsymbol{\beta}})\right\rVert_2^2\\
&=\widehat{{\boldsymbol{e}}}^{\top}\widehat{{\boldsymbol{e}}}
   +\widehat{{\boldsymbol{e}}}^{\top}{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}
      (\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-{\boldsymbol{\beta}})
   +(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-{\boldsymbol{\beta}})^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top
      \widehat{{\boldsymbol{e}}}\\
&\qquad
   +(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-{\boldsymbol{\beta}})^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}
      (\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-{\boldsymbol{\beta}})\\
&=\operatorname{SSE}
   +2\widehat{{\boldsymbol{e}}}^{\top}{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}
      (\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-{\boldsymbol{\beta}})
   +(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-{\boldsymbol{\beta}})^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}
      (\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-{\boldsymbol{\beta}}).
\end{align*} The normal equations imply

{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top\widehat{{\boldsymbol{e}}}
={\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top({\boldsymbol{y}}-{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}})
={\boldsymbol{0}},

so \widehat{{\boldsymbol{e}}}^{\top}{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}={\boldsymbol{0}}^\top and the cross-term is zero. Consequently,

Theorem 1 (Least-squares decomposition) 
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{SSE}({\boldsymbol{\beta}})
&=\operatorname{SSE}
+({\boldsymbol{\beta}}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}})^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}
  ({\boldsymbol{\beta}}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}})\\
&=\operatorname{SSE}
  +\lVert{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}\rVert_{{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}}^2\\
&=\operatorname{SSE}
  +\lVert{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}({\boldsymbol{\beta}}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}})\rVert_2^2.
\end{aligned}}
\qquad(17) The second term is nonnegative because {\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}} is positive definite. This both proves that OLS minimises the SSE and separates the posterior’s coefficient uncertainty from its fitted residual variation. Geometrically, \widehat{{\boldsymbol{e}}} is orthogonal to the column space of {\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}, while {\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-{\boldsymbol{\beta}}) lies in that column space. The theorem is therefore the Pythagorean theorem for those two orthogonal components of the candidate residual vector.
Article: Least-squares decomposition →

Recognising the posterior distributions
Substituting Equation 17 into Equation 15 yields 
\begin{aligned}
p({\boldsymbol{\beta}},\sigma^2\mid{\boldsymbol{y}},{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}})
\propto{}& (\sigma^2)^{-(n/2+1)}
\exp\!\left\{-\frac{\operatorname{SSE}}{2\sigma^2}\right\}\\
&\times
\exp\!\left\{-\frac{
  ({\boldsymbol{\beta}}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}})^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}
  ({\boldsymbol{\beta}}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}})}{2\sigma^2}\right\}.
\end{aligned}
\qquad(18) In norm notation, the quadratic exponent in the second factor is

-\frac{
\lVert{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}\rVert_{{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}}^2}
{2\sigma^2}.

Its level sets are ellipsoids centred at \widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}; the {\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}} geometry determines their directions and relative widths. These two factors are an inverse-Gamma-looking variance term and a multivariate-normal coefficient term. That recognition is correct in spirit, but the normalising power of \sigma^2 determines whether the variance distribution is conditional or marginal. We therefore restore that power explicitly in the next two steps.

Conditional posterior of the coefficients
Holding \sigma^2 fixed, the terms involving {\boldsymbol{\beta}} have a multivariate normal kernel. In fact,

Proposition 3 (Conditional coefficient posterior) 
\boxed{
{\boldsymbol{\beta}}\mid\sigma^2,{\boldsymbol{y}},{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}
\sim\operatorname{N}_p\!\left(
\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}},
\sigma^2({\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}})^{-1}\right).}
\qquad(19) To check the normalising factor, set {\color{blue}\boldsymbol{C}}=({\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}})^{-1}. Then

|\sigma^2{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{C}}|^{-1/2}
=(\sigma^2)^{-p/2}|{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}|^{1/2},

and (\sigma^2{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{C}})^{-1}
=\sigma^{-2}{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}, exactly matching Equation 18.
