Marginal posterior of the variance
Integrating the coefficient kernel over \mathbb{R}^p contributes \begin{align*}
&\int_{\mathbb{R}^p}
\exp\!\left\{-\frac{
  ({\boldsymbol{\beta}}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}})^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}
  ({\boldsymbol{\beta}}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}})}{2\sigma^2}\right\}\,\mathrm{d}{\boldsymbol{\beta}}\\
&\qquad=(2\pi\sigma^2)^{p/2}
|{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}|^{-1/2}.
\end{align*} The determinant does not depend on \sigma^2, while the power (\sigma^2)^{p/2} changes the variance kernel to \begin{align*}
p(\sigma^2\mid{\boldsymbol{y}},{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}})
&\propto
(\sigma^2)^{-(n/2+1)}(\sigma^2)^{p/2}
\exp\!\left\{-\frac{\operatorname{SSE}}{2\sigma^2}\right\}\\
&=(\sigma^2)^{-\{(n-p)/2+1\}}
\exp\!\left\{-\frac{\operatorname{SSE}/2}{\sigma^2}\right\}.
\end{align*} Under the book’s inverse-Gamma convention,

Proposition 4 (Marginal variance posterior) 
\boxed{
\sigma^2\mid{\boldsymbol{y}},{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}
\sim\operatorname{InvGamma}\!\left(
\frac{n-p}{2},\frac{\operatorname{SSE}}2\right).}
\qquad(20) The p/2 from the normalising constant is subtracted from n/2; it is not added. Equivalently, 
\frac{\operatorname{SSE}}{\sigma^2}\mid{\boldsymbol{y}},{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}
\sim\chi^2_{n-p}.
\qquad(21) If n&gt;p+2, the posterior mean exists and is

\mathbb{E}(\sigma^2\mid{\boldsymbol{y}},{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}})
=\frac{\operatorname{SSE}}{n-p-2}
=\frac{n-p}{n-p-2}s^2.

The posterior mode is \operatorname{SSE}/(n-p+2). Thus the unbiased estimator s^2 is generally neither the posterior mean nor the posterior mode; calling any one quantity “the estimate” implicitly chooses a loss function.
