Marginal posterior of the coefficients
Let \nu=n-p, {\color{blue}\boldsymbol{C}}=({\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}})^{-1}, and \begin{align*}
Q({\boldsymbol{\beta}})
&=({\boldsymbol{\beta}}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}})^\top
{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{C}}^{-1}({\boldsymbol{\beta}}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}})\\
&=\lVert{\boldsymbol{\beta}}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}\rVert_{{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{C}}^{-1}}^2\\
&=\lVert{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}({\boldsymbol{\beta}}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}})\rVert_2^2.
\end{align*} Thus Q({\boldsymbol{\beta}}) is squared distance from the least-squares centre in the coefficient geometry induced by the design. Multiplying the normal density in Equation 19 by the inverse-Gamma density in Equation 20 and integrating gives \begin{align*}
p({\boldsymbol{\beta}}\mid{\boldsymbol{y}},{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}})
&\propto\int_0^\infty
(\sigma^2)^{-\{(p+\nu)/2+1\}}
\exp\!\left\{-\frac{\operatorname{SSE}+Q({\boldsymbol{\beta}})}
                         {2\sigma^2}\right\}\,\mathrm{d}\sigma^2.
\end{align*} For a,c&gt;0,

\int_0^\infty u^{-(a+1)}e^{-c/u}\,\mathrm{d} u=\Gamma(a)c^{-a}.

Using a=(p+\nu)/2 and c=\{\operatorname{SSE}+Q({\boldsymbol{\beta}})\}/2 therefore yields \begin{align*}
p({\boldsymbol{\beta}}\mid{\boldsymbol{y}},{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}})
&\propto
\{\operatorname{SSE}+Q({\boldsymbol{\beta}})\}^{-(p+\nu)/2}\\
&\propto
\left\{1+\frac{Q({\boldsymbol{\beta}})}{\nu s^2}\right\}^{-(p+\nu)/2},
\end{align*} because \operatorname{SSE}=\nu s^2. This is the multivariate Student distribution

Corollary 2 (Marginal Student posterior) 
\boxed{
{\boldsymbol{\beta}}\mid{\boldsymbol{y}},{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}
\sim t_{n-p}\!\left(
\widehat{\boldsymbol{\beta}},
s^2({\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}^\top{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}})^{-1}\right),}
\qquad(22) where the displayed matrix is the multivariate t scale matrix. In particular, 
\beta_j\mid{\boldsymbol{y}},{\color{blue}\boldsymbol{X}}
\sim t_{n-p}\!\left(\widehat\beta_j,s\sqrt{c_{jj}}\right),
\qquad(23) so an equal-tailed 100(1-\alpha)\% credible interval is 
\widehat\beta_j\ \pm\ t_{1-\alpha/2,n-p}s\sqrt{c_{jj}}.
\qquad(24) Its endpoints coincide with the usual classical interval under this reference prior. The interpretations do not coincide: the Bayesian statement concerns posterior uncertainty about \beta_j for the observed data, whereas the frequentist statement concerns repeated-sample coverage by a random interval.
