Topic: Exponential families and conjugacy · more questions on this topic
Source: Practice set, question 2 of 59 ‹ Q1 Q3 ›

At each of n locations, researchers wait until they capture the mth animal. Let X_i denote the waiting time at location i, and suppose X_i\mid\theta
  \overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}\operatorname{Gamma}(m,\theta),
  \qquad i=1,\ldots,n, where the Gamma distribution is parameterised by shape m and scale \theta, so that f(x_i\mid\theta)
  =\frac{x_i^{m-1}}{\Gamma(m)\theta^m}
   \exp\left(-\frac{x_i}{\theta}\right),
  \qquad x_i&gt;0. Assume the prior distribution \theta\sim\operatorname{InvGamma}(\alpha_0,\beta_0), with density p(\theta)
  =\frac{\beta_0^{\alpha_0}}{\Gamma(\alpha_0)}
   \theta^{-\alpha_0-1}
   \exp\left(-\frac{\beta_0}{\theta}\right),
  \qquad \theta&gt;0.
(a) Write down the likelihood L(\theta;{\boldsymbol{x}}), up to proportionality in \theta.
(b) Find the posterior distribution of \theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}.
(c) State the posterior mean \mathbb{E}(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}) and the condition under which it exists.
(d) Suppose \widehat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}=\frac{\bar{x}}{m}. Show that the posterior mean is a weighted average of the prior mean and the MLE. Verify that the weights sum to one and briefly interpret the result.
(e) Examine the limiting behaviour of the posterior mean:
(i) What happens to the weights as m\to\infty?
(ii) What happens, formally, as the prior becomes increasingly weak, represented by \alpha_0,\beta_0\to0?






Worked answer (click to show)





Definitions. Here \operatorname{Gamma}(m,\theta) uses shape m and scale \theta, not rate. Write \bar x=n^{-1}\sum_i x_i and \widehat\theta_{\mathrm{MLE}}=\bar x/m. Our inverse-Gamma convention is p(\theta)\propto\theta^{-a-1}e^{-b/\theta} for \theta&gt;0 and a,b&gt;0. The observations are conditionally iid, m&gt;0 is known, and the prior has \alpha_0,\beta_0&gt;0. Conditions for the means to exist are checked below.

(a) Likelihood
Independence gives \begin{aligned}
  L(\theta;{\boldsymbol{x}})
  &=\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i\mid\theta)\\
  &=\prod_{i=1}^n
    \frac{x_i^{m-1}}{\Gamma(m)\theta^m}
    \exp\left(-\frac{x_i}{\theta}\right).
\end{aligned} Discarding factors that do not depend on \theta, \begin{aligned}
  L(\theta;{\boldsymbol{x}})
  &\propto
    \prod_{i=1}^n
    \theta^{-m}\exp\left(-\frac{x_i}{\theta}\right)\\
  &=\theta^{-nm}
    \exp\left(-\frac{\sum_{i=1}^n x_i}{\theta}\right).
\end{aligned} Therefore, \boxed{
    L(\theta;{\boldsymbol{x}})
    \propto
    \theta^{-nm}
    \exp\left(-\frac{\sum_{i=1}^n x_i}{\theta}\right)
  }.


(b) Posterior distribution
The inverse-Gamma prior has kernel p(\theta)
  \propto
  \theta^{-\alpha_0-1}
  \exp\left(-\frac{\beta_0}{\theta}\right). By Bayes’ rule, \begin{aligned}
  p(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
  &\propto L(\theta;{\boldsymbol{x}})p(\theta)\\
  &\propto
    \theta^{-nm}
    \exp\left(-\frac{\sum_{i=1}^n x_i}{\theta}\right)
    \theta^{-\alpha_0-1}
    \exp\left(-\frac{\beta_0}{\theta}\right)\\
  &\propto
    \theta^{-(\alpha_0+nm)-1}
    \exp\left(
      -\frac{\beta_0+\sum_{i=1}^n x_i}{\theta}
    \right).
\end{aligned} This is an inverse-Gamma kernel, so \boxed{
    \theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}
    \sim
    \operatorname{InvGamma}\left(
      \alpha_0+nm,
      \beta_0+\sum_{i=1}^n x_i
    \right)
  }. Equivalently, the conjugate update is \boxed{
    \alpha_0\longmapsto\alpha_0+nm,
    \qquad
    \beta_0\longmapsto\beta_0+\sum_{i=1}^n x_i
  }.


(c) Posterior mean
Under the parameterisation used here, if Z\sim\operatorname{InvGamma}(a,b), then \mathbb{E}(Z)=\frac{b}{a-1},
  \qquad a&gt;1. It follows that \boxed{
    \mathbb{E}(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
    =\frac{\beta_0+\sum_{i=1}^n x_i}
           {\alpha_0+nm-1}
  },
  \qquad
  \boxed{\alpha_0+nm&gt;1}.


(d) Weighted-average interpretation
Provided \alpha_0&gt;1, the prior mean is \mathbb{E}(\theta)=\frac{\beta_0}{\alpha_0-1}, and hence \beta_0=(\alpha_0-1)\mathbb{E}(\theta). Also, \sum_{i=1}^n x_i
  =n\bar{x}
  =nm\widehat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}. Substituting these identities into the posterior mean gives \begin{aligned}
  \mathbb{E}(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
  &=\frac{(\alpha_0-1)\mathbb{E}(\theta)
           +nm\widehat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}}
          {\alpha_0+nm-1}\\
  &=\frac{\alpha_0-1}{\alpha_0+nm-1}\mathbb{E}(\theta)
    +\frac{nm}{\alpha_0+nm-1}
     \widehat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}.
\end{aligned} Thus, \boxed{
    \mathbb{E}(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
    =w_0\mathbb{E}(\theta)+w_n\widehat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}
  }, where w_0=\frac{\alpha_0-1}{\alpha_0+nm-1},
  \qquad
  w_n=\frac{nm}{\alpha_0+nm-1}. The weights are non-negative when \alpha_0&gt;1, and w_0+w_n
  =\frac{\alpha_0-1+nm}{\alpha_0+nm-1}
  =1. The posterior mean is therefore a convex combination of the prior mean and the MLE. This is Bayesian shrinkage: the posterior estimate balances prior information against information in the observed data.


(e) Limiting behaviour

(i) As m\to\infty.
For fixed n and fixed prior hyperparameters, w_0=\frac{\alpha_0-1}{\alpha_0+nm-1}\longrightarrow0,
  \qquad
  w_n=\frac{nm}{\alpha_0+nm-1}\longrightarrow1. Hence the prior receives vanishing weight. If the MLE remains bounded (as it does in probability under sampling at a fixed true scale), then \boxed{
    \mathbb{E}(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
    -\widehat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}
    \longrightarrow0
  }. In this sense, as the data contribution dominates, the posterior mean approaches the data-based estimate.


(ii) As the prior becomes weak.
Starting from the direct expression for the posterior mean and formally letting \alpha_0,\beta_0\to0 gives \begin{aligned}
  \mathbb{E}(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
  &\longrightarrow
    \frac{\sum_{i=1}^n x_i}{nm-1}\\
  &=\frac{nm}{nm-1}\widehat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}},
  \qquad nm&gt;1.
\end{aligned} Therefore, \boxed{
    \mathbb{E}(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
    \longrightarrow
    \frac{nm}{nm-1}\widehat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}
    \approx\widehat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}
  }, with the approximation becoming exact as nm\to\infty.
This is a formal limiting argument. A proper inverse-Gamma prior has \alpha_0,\beta_0&gt;0, and its mean exists only when \alpha_0&gt;1. Thus the path \alpha_0,\beta_0\to0 does not remain within the class of proper priors having finite means; the limit should be taken from the posterior expression, not from the weighted-average representation involving \mathbb{E}(\theta).

Key result. The full progression is \boxed{
    L(\theta;{\boldsymbol{x}})
    \longrightarrow
    p(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
    \longrightarrow
    \mathbb{E}(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
    \longrightarrow
    w_0\mathbb{E}(\theta)+w_n\widehat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}
    \longrightarrow
    \widehat{\theta}_{\mathrm{MLE}}
  }, as the data contribution dominates.

Topic: Markov chains, Gibbs sampling, and Metropolis–Hastings · more questions on this topic
Source: Practice set, question 14 of 59 ‹ Q13 Q15 ›

[4 marks]
Suppose two unknown parameters have joint posterior density p(\theta_1,\theta_2\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}). Explain the distinction between the marginal posterior p(\theta_1\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}) and the full conditional posterior p(\theta_1\mid\theta_2,{\boldsymbol{x}}). In your answer, state what is being conditioned on and describe how each density can be obtained from the joint posterior.






Worked answer (click to show)





Definitions. Let {\boldsymbol{x}} be the observed data and p(\theta_1,\theta_2\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}) a proper joint posterior. A marginal integrates out an unreported coordinate; a full conditional holds every other coordinate fixed. Conditional-density ratios below are used only where p(\theta_2\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})&gt;0. The symbol u is a dummy integration variable; whether \theta_2 is integrated out or held fixed distinguishes the two calculations.
The marginal posterior p(\theta_1\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}) conditions only on the observed data. It describes uncertainty about \theta_1 while averaging over all possible values of \theta_2. It is obtained by integrating \theta_2 out of the joint posterior: \boxed{
p(\theta_1\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})
=\int p(\theta_1,\theta_2\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})\,d\theta_2.
}
The full conditional posterior p(\theta_1\mid\theta_2,{\boldsymbol{x}}) conditions on both the data and a specified value of \theta_2. By the definition of conditional density, \begin{aligned}
p(\theta_1\mid\theta_2,{\boldsymbol{x}})
&=\frac{p(\theta_1,\theta_2\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})}
{p(\theta_2\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})}\\
&=\boxed{
\frac{p(\theta_1,\theta_2\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})}
{\int p(u,\theta_2\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})\,du}
}.
\end{aligned} Equivalently, when viewed as a function of \theta_1, p(\theta_1\mid\theta_2,{\boldsymbol{x}})
\propto p(\theta_1,\theta_2\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}), because the normalising denominator is constant with respect to \theta_1. Thus marginalisation averages over the other parameter, whereas conditioning holds it fixed. Full conditionals are the distributions used for Gibbs updates.

Topic: The Beta–Binomial model · more questions on this topic
Source: Practice set, question 42 of 59 ‹ Q41 Q43 ›

[12 marks]
Suppose X\mid\theta\sim\operatorname{Binomial}(n,\theta). Define Z=\log\frac{\theta}{1-\theta}, and assign the prior Z\sim N(1,1).
(a) Derive the prior density of \theta, including the Jacobian. [3 marks]
(b) Find the posterior kernel p(\theta\mid x). Is this prior conjugate? [2 marks]
(c) Derive the posterior kernel p(z\mid x). [3 marks]
(d) Construct a random-walk Metropolis–Hastings algorithm targeting p(z\mid x), using Z^*\mid Z^{(t)}=z\sim N(z,s^2). Explain how its output can estimate \mathbb{E}(\theta\mid x). [4 marks]






Worked answer (click to show)





Definitions. The sampling parameter is 0&lt;\theta&lt;1, and its logit coordinate is z=\log\{\theta/(1-\theta)\}. The prior is Z\sim N(1,1), where the second argument is variance. For an invertible transformation, densities obey p_\theta(\theta)=p_Z(z(\theta))|dz/d\theta|. A kernel is always defined relative to its coordinate and reference measure; here these are d\theta or dz.
(a) Prior density of \theta. The inverse transformation is \theta=g(z)=\frac{e^z}{1+e^z},\qquad z\in\mathbb R, and \frac{dz}{d\theta}
=\frac{d}{d\theta}\{\log\theta-\log(1-\theta)\}
=\frac1{\theta(1-\theta)}. Since Z\sim N(1,1), the change-of-variable formula gives \boxed{
p(\theta)
=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}\,\theta(1-\theta)}
\exp\left[-\frac12\left\{\log\frac{\theta}{1-\theta}-1\right\}^2\right],
\quad 0&lt;\theta&lt;1.
} The density is zero outside (0,1). This is a logit-normal prior.
(b) Posterior kernel for \theta. For an observed count x\in\{0,\ldots,n\}, L(\theta;x)=\binom nx\theta^x(1-\theta)^{n-x}. Multiplication by the prior gives \boxed{
p(\theta\mid x)\propto
\theta^{x-1}(1-\theta)^{n-x-1}
\exp\left[-\frac12\left\{\log\frac{\theta}{1-\theta}-1\right\}^2\right],
\quad 0&lt;\theta&lt;1.
} This prior is not conjugate: the posterior does not in general remain logit-normal. In particular, its logit-transformed posterior is not Normal, as the following kernel shows.
(c) Posterior kernel for z. Substituting \theta=g(z) in the likelihood, \theta^x(1-\theta)^{n-x}
=\frac{e^{xz}}{(1+e^z)^n}. Multiplying by the Normal prior density for z yields \boxed{
p(z\mid x)\propto
\exp\left\{xz-n\log(1+e^z)-\frac12(z-1)^2\right\},
\quad z\in\mathbb R.
} Alternatively, transforming the posterior from part (b) requires |d\theta/dz|=\theta(1-\theta), which cancels the prior’s Jacobian factor. Simply substituting \theta=g(z) into a density in \theta without this Jacobian would give the wrong density in z.
(d) Random-walk Metropolis–Hastings sampler. Define the log posterior kernel h(z)=xz-n\log(1+e^z)-\frac12(z-1)^2. The proposal density is symmetric because q(z^*\mid z)
=\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi s^2}}\exp\left\{-\frac{(z^*-z)^2}{2s^2}\right\}
=q(z\mid z^*). Thus the acceptance probability is A(z,z^*)=\min\{1,\exp[h(z^*)-h(z)]\}. Equivalently, the log of the untruncated ratio is \begin{aligned}
\Delta=h(z^*)-h(z)
&=x(z^*-z)-n\{\log(1+e^{z^*})-\log(1+e^z)\}\\
&\quad-\frac12\{(z^*-1)^2-(z-1)^2\}.
\end{aligned}
Pseudocode. Choose any z^{(0)}\in\mathbb R and a fixed s&gt;0. For t=0,\ldots,T-1:
1. Propose z^*\sim N(z^{(t)},s^2).
2. Calculate \Delta=h(z^*)-h(z^{(t)}).
3. Independently draw U\sim\operatorname{Uniform}(0,1).
4. If \log U\le\min\{0,\Delta\}, set z^{(t+1)}=z^*; otherwise set z^{(t+1)}=z^{(t)}.
This Markov chain has p(z\mid x) as its equilibrium density. Initial draws from an arbitrary start are not automatically posterior draws, and successive draws are generally dependent.
Estimating the requested posterior mean. After discarding a suitable initial burn-in of B iterations, transform each retained state and average: \theta^{(t)}=g(z^{(t)}),
\qquad
\boxed{
\widehat{\mathbb{E}(\theta\mid x)}
=\frac1{T-B}\sum_{t=B+1}^T\frac{e^{z^{(t)}}}{1+e^{z^{(t)}}}
}. With adequate convergence and a sufficiently long run, the ergodic average estimates the posterior expectation. Assess mixing and Monte Carlo error; the retained values are not independent.
For stable numerical evaluation, use \log(1+e^z)=\max\{0,z\}+\log(1+e^{-|z|}) and a stable logistic function.

Key result. Average the transformed draws. In general, \mathbb{E}\{g(Z)\mid x\}\ne g\{\mathbb{E}(Z\mid x)\}, so transforming the average of the z draws does not estimate the same quantity.

Topic: Bayesian hypotheses and model comparison · more questions on this topic
Source: Practice set, question 44 of 59 ‹ Q43 Q45 ›

[10 marks]
Suppose X\mid\theta\sim\operatorname{Binomial}(n,\theta). Compare M_0:\theta=\frac12,
\qquad M_1:\theta\sim\operatorname{Beta}(a,b), with a,b&gt;0 and equal prior model probabilities.
You may use B(a,b)=\int_0^1t^{a-1}(1-t)^{b-1}\,dt.
(a) Derive \mathbb{P}(X=x\mid M_0) and \mathbb{P}(X=x\mid M_1). [4 marks]
(b) Obtain the Bayes factor BF_{01} and the posterior probability \mathbb{P}(M_0\mid X=x). [3 marks]
(c) A student argues:

“Under a continuous Beta posterior, \mathbb{P}(\theta=1/2\mid x)=0, so Bayesian inference always rejects a fair coin.”

Explain the error. [3 marks]






Worked answer (click to show)





Definitions. M_0 fixes \theta=1/2 and M_1 assigns a \operatorname{Beta}(a,b) prior, with a,b&gt;0. Its density is \theta^{a-1}(1-\theta)^{b-1}/B(a,b), where B(a,b)=\int_0^1u^{a-1}(1-u)^{b-1}\,du. BF_{01} is the marginal-likelihood ratio favouring M_0. Prior model probabilities are equal; a model probability is distinct from a point probability within the continuous alternative.
(a) Marginal likelihoods. Under M_0, \theta=1/2 is fixed, so \boxed{\mathbb{P}(X=x\mid M_0)=\binom nx2^{-n}.} Under M_1, average the likelihood over the Beta prior: \begin{aligned}
\mathbb{P}(X=x\mid M_1)
&=\int_0^1\binom nx\theta^x(1-\theta)^{n-x}
\frac{\theta^{a-1}(1-\theta)^{b-1}}{B(a,b)}\,d\theta\\
&=\frac{\binom nx}{B(a,b)}
\int_0^1\theta^{a+x-1}(1-\theta)^{b+n-x-1}\,d\theta\\
&=\boxed{\binom nx\frac{B(a+x,b+n-x)}{B(a,b)}}.
\end{aligned}
(b) Bayes factor and posterior model probability. The Bayes factor in favour of M_0 over M_1 is \boxed{
BF_{01}
=\frac{\mathbb{P}(X=x\mid M_0)}{\mathbb{P}(X=x\mid M_1)}
=2^{-n}\frac{B(a,b)}{B(a+x,b+n-x)}.
} The binomial coefficients cancel in the ratio, but belong in the individual marginal likelihoods. Equal prior model probabilities give prior odds one, so posterior odds equal BF_{01}. Hence \boxed{
\mathbb{P}(M_0\mid X=x)
=\frac{BF_{01}}{1+BF_{01}}
=\frac{2^{-n}B(a,b)}{2^{-n}B(a,b)+B(a+x,b+n-x)}.
}
(c) Error in the student’s argument. Under M_1 alone, \theta\mid x,M_1\sim\operatorname{Beta}(a+x,b+n-x),
\qquad \mathbb{P}(\theta=1/2\mid x,M_1)=0, because this posterior is continuous. Every individual point has probability zero under it; that fact is not a test rejecting that particular value.
The model comparison instead assigns positive prior probability to the separate point-null model M_0. Both marginal likelihoods are positive for valid data, so \mathbb{P}(M_0\mid x)&gt;0. In the combined model, \mathbb{P}(\theta=1/2\mid x)=\mathbb{P}(M_0\mid x), since M_0 places all its probability at 1/2 and M_1 contributes no point mass there. The student has confused a probability conditional on M_1 with the probability after allowing both models.

Topic: Markov chains, Gibbs sampling, and Metropolis–Hastings · more questions on this topic
Source: Practice set, question 50 of 59 ‹ Q49 Q51 ›

[8 marks]
A parameter \theta\in\mathbb R has posterior density \pi(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})\propto L(\theta;{\boldsymbol{x}})p(\theta). Assume the likelihood and prior are positive at the current and proposed states. From the current state \theta, propose \theta'=\theta+\varepsilon,\qquad\varepsilon\sim N(0,s^2), where s^2&gt;0 is fixed.
(a) Write the general Metropolis–Hastings acceptance probability, including the proposal densities. [2 marks]
(b) Simplify it for this proposal. State and justify the precise equality that permits cancellation of the proposal densities. [2 marks]
(c) Suppose the prior is standard Cauchy: p(\theta)=\frac1{\pi(1+\theta^2)}. Write the log of the untruncated acceptance ratio explicitly in terms of the log-likelihood and the current and proposed states. [2 marks]
(d) Explain the numerical advantage of using logarithms, and state how to implement the accept/reject decision using U\sim\operatorname{Uniform}(0,1) without exponentiating the log ratio. [2 marks]






Worked answer (click to show)





Definitions. Let \ell(\theta)=\log L(\theta;{\boldsymbol{x}}) and let q(\theta'\mid\theta) be the proposal density. The untruncated MH ratio is r; the acceptance probability is \min(1,r). The standard Cauchy prior has density 1/\{\pi(1+\theta^2)\} on \mathbb R. The fixed Normal random walk uses variance s^2&gt;0, and likelihood and prior are positive at both states.
(a) General acceptance probability. Let q(\theta'\mid\theta) denote the proposal density. The general rule is \boxed{
A(\theta,\theta')
=\min\left\{1,
\frac{\pi(\theta'\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})q(\theta\mid\theta')}
     {\pi(\theta\mid{\boldsymbol{x}})q(\theta'\mid\theta)}\right\}.
} Substituting the posterior kernel, the untruncated ratio is r(\theta,\theta')
=\frac{L(\theta';{\boldsymbol{x}})p(\theta')q(\theta\mid\theta')}
       {L(\theta;{\boldsymbol{x}})p(\theta)q(\theta'\mid\theta)}. The posterior’s unknown normalising constant cancels between numerator and denominator.
(b) Symmetric Normal random walk. For the given proposal, q(\theta'\mid\theta)
=\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi s^2}}
\exp\left\{-\frac{(\theta'-\theta)^2}{2s^2}\right\}. Since (\theta'-\theta)^2=(\theta-\theta')^2 and the same fixed s^2 is used in both directions, \boxed{q(\theta'\mid\theta)=q(\theta\mid\theta').} It is this equality of the forward and reverse density values that permits cancellation. The proposal distributions centred at \theta and \theta' are not generally identical distributions. Thus \boxed{
A(\theta,\theta')
=\min\left\{1,\frac{L(\theta';{\boldsymbol{x}})p(\theta')}
                         {L(\theta;{\boldsymbol{x}})p(\theta)}\right\}.
}
(c) Log of the untruncated ratio under the Cauchy prior. Write \ell(\theta)=\log L(\theta;{\boldsymbol{x}}). Since \frac{p(\theta')}{p(\theta)}
=\frac{1+\theta^2}{1+(\theta')^2}, we obtain \boxed{
\log r(\theta,\theta')
=\ell(\theta')-\ell(\theta)
+\log(1+\theta^2)-\log\{1+(\theta')^2\}.
} This quantity may be positive. It is not yet \log A(\theta,\theta'); the log acceptance probability is \min\{0,\log r(\theta,\theta')\}.
(d) Stable accept/reject decision. Likelihoods can be products of many small numbers, causing underflow; ratios of very large or very small values can also overflow or lose precision. Working directly with log-likelihoods converts products into sums and ratios into differences, avoiding the need to form the raw likelihoods or acceptance ratio.
Let \Delta=\ell(\theta')-\ell(\theta)
+\log(1+\theta^2)-\log\{1+(\theta')^2\}. Draw U\sim\operatorname{Uniform}(0,1) independently of the proposal. Since logarithms are increasing, U\le\min\{1,e^\Delta\}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\boxed{\log U\le\min\{0,\Delta\}.} If the inequality holds, accept \theta' as the next state; otherwise retain \theta. No exponentiation of \Delta is required. Equivalently, accept every proposal with \Delta\ge0, and otherwise accept when \log U\le\Delta.
